1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,960
 I was asked a pretty fair question while I was developing the Boost container.

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:14,440
 It's like why don't you add a way to load in all the configurations so that people don't

3
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:17,200
 lose their stuff?

4
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:27,040
 The reason is very direct because that's not how containers are designed to be used.

5
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:35,260
 They can be used that way, but the way containers are meant to be used in the enterprise universally

6
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:43,400
 is as a singular ephemeral meaning that they live and die all the time, and that what's

7
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:49,960
 in them is in them, that they, any information that needs to be passed into them is passed

8
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:57,440
 in through environment variables, sometimes amount points, but almost not ever.

9
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:01,960
 In case of Kubernetes, there's amount points and config maps and all that, so you can put

10
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:10,840
 a file in there, but the idea of it specifically as a beginner of thinking about a container

11
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:16,240
 as a thing that's going to contain, it's like your laptop Linux and you're going to be able

12
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:19,680
 to change all your configs and everything and load them from somewhere.

13
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:21,440
 I think is a mistake.

14
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:26,280
 I think it's actually a bad way to think about containers for a beginner.

15
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:32,960
 Beginners need to immediately think about containers as going away, as living and dying.

16
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:37,880
 When they type exit, that thing goes away, right, and even though we violate that a little

17
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:42,040
 bit for the boost here to be, to reattach to the container that's still running or actually

18
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:46,440
 not running and start it up and then reuse it because it's got stuff inside of it, not

19
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:52,200
 being able to throw it away, and we do do that to get them started, but it's more important

20
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:58,480
 that the user, especially the beginner, gets used to saving out whatever it is that they're

21
00:01:58,480 --> 00:01:59,480
 doing.

22
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:05,200
 So whatever work they're doing in their container, that work in order to be stateful, in order

23
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:12,400
 to persist beyond the life of the container, should be saved someplace else.

24
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:18,660
 But almost always, that is not in something that has been mounted in the container.

25
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:24,280
 It's almost always communicated through a network connection.

26
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:28,880
 And in our case for the beginner boost, that means storing it in Git.

27
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:35,760
 That means from the very first day almost, we are going to start practicing saving content,

28
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:37,960
 saving everything we do in Git.

29
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:42,040
 And if somebody has an experience during the beginner boost where they accidentally delete

30
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:45,800
 their container and lose all their work, we want that to happen.

31
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:49,560
 The reason we want it to happen now is because we want them to understand that you should

32
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:53,200
 never, ever preserve state in a container.

33
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:58,080
 And I guarantee you, there's somebody watching this, please leave a comment below, if you

34
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:03,680
 have ever accidentally made the false assumption when you're in a container, that something

35
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:08,240
 you changed was going to live on beyond the container and then lost your work.

36
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:09,240
 I've done it.

37
00:03:09,240 --> 00:03:13,520
 I think a lot of you have too, it's a very tough lesson to learn, but it's an important

38
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:15,440
 lesson to learn early on.

39
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:21,280
 And the way to do that is to not encourage people to mount volumes and mount other things

40
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:24,840
 into the container, which you will eventually learn about doing, but not now.

41
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:28,120
 Right now you want to learn that it doesn't exist unless they get saved in Git.

